From: Leo Huang <leohu@nvidia.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 23:57:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 6.12 1/1] i2c: ast2600: fix NULL deref in the AST2700 buffer-mode target IRQ

Upstream-Status: Pending [downstream fix for an Aspeed defect, not yet fixed on aspeed-master-v6.12]

ast2700_i2c_target_packet_buff_irq() handles slave transfers in pool-buffer
mode, where received bytes are read out of the controller's on-chip buffer with
readb(i2c_bus->buf_base + 0x30 + i). Three call sites were missed when the
function was derived from the DMA handler and still pass
&i2c_bus->target_dma_buf[], which ast2600_i2c_init() only allocates when
i2c_bus->mode == DMA_MODE. In buffer mode that pointer is NULL, so the slave
backend is handed a NULL val and dereferences it:

  Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 0
  pc : i2c_slave_eeprom_slave_cb+0xa4/0x12c
  lr : i2c_slave_event+0x28/0x100
  Call trace:
   i2c_slave_eeprom_slave_cb+0xa4/0x12c (P)
   i2c_slave_event+0x28/0x100
   ast2700_i2c_target_packet_buff_irq+0x1028/0x1640
   ast2600_i2c_bus_irq+0x1b8/0x6a0
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Oops: Fatal exception in interrupt

The one that fires in practice is the I2C_SLAVE_WRITE_RECEIVED loop in the
WAIT_TX_DMA | SLAVE_MATCH | RX_DONE case, i.e. a repeated-start write-then-read
against a registered slave. The neighbouring case with STOP set was converted
correctly, so a plain write completes fine and only the turnaround crashes.

Reproduced on vr-nvl-hmc-ast2700 running an i2c-slave-eeprom at 0x4f: with the
peer BMC polling it the board panics within ~75s, and with the poll stopped it
runs indefinitely with the same target registered.

The other two sites are I2C_SLAVE_WRITE_REQUESTED, where i2c-slave-eeprom
ignores val, so they are latent; ssif_bmc and i2c-slave-mqueue both dereference
it and would crash the same way.

Fix all three to match the twelve sibling call sites in the same function.

This is a downstream fix for an upstream defect introduced by f68df26148bd
("i2c: ast2600: introduce buffer transferring support to AST2700") and still
present on aspeed-master-v6.12 and v6.18. It is deliberately written to be the
same change Aspeed would make, so it should drop out cleanly once they fix it.

Signed-off-by: Leo Huang <leohu@nvidia.com>
---
 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-ast2600.c | 10 ++++------
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-ast2600.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-ast2600.c
index f893ddfef..dbb5342f3 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-ast2600.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-ast2600.c
@@ -1273,8 +1273,7 @@ static void ast2700_i2c_target_packet_buff_irq(struct ast2600_i2c_bus *i2c_bus,
 			if (!i2c_bus->target)
 				ast2700_i2c_get_target(i2c_bus, sirq_log >> SLAVE_ADDR_SHIFT);
 			if (i2c_bus->target) {
-				i2c_slave_event(i2c_bus->target, I2C_SLAVE_WRITE_REQUESTED,
-						&i2c_bus->target_dma_buf[0]);
+				i2c_slave_event(i2c_bus->target, I2C_SLAVE_WRITE_REQUESTED, &value);
 			}
 			sirq_log = readl(i2c_bus->reg_base + AST2700_I2CC_SIRQ_LOG);
 		}
@@ -1401,8 +1400,8 @@ static void ast2700_i2c_target_packet_buff_irq(struct ast2600_i2c_bus *i2c_bus,
 							  AST2600_I2CS_DMA_LEN_STS));
 		for (i = 0; i < target_rx_len; i++) {
 			if (i2c_bus->target) {
-				i2c_slave_event(i2c_bus->target, I2C_SLAVE_WRITE_RECEIVED,
-						&i2c_bus->target_dma_buf[i]);
+				value = readb(i2c_bus->buf_base + 0x30 + i);
+				i2c_slave_event(i2c_bus->target, I2C_SLAVE_WRITE_RECEIVED, &value);
 			}
 		}
 		sirq_log = readl(i2c_bus->reg_base + AST2700_I2CC_SIRQ_LOG);
@@ -1453,8 +1452,7 @@ static void ast2700_i2c_target_packet_buff_irq(struct ast2600_i2c_bus *i2c_bus,
 		if (!i2c_bus->target)
 			ast2700_i2c_get_target(i2c_bus, sirq_log >> SLAVE_ADDR_SHIFT);
 		if (i2c_bus->target) {
-			i2c_slave_event(i2c_bus->target, I2C_SLAVE_WRITE_REQUESTED,
-					&i2c_bus->target_dma_buf[0]);
+			i2c_slave_event(i2c_bus->target, I2C_SLAVE_WRITE_REQUESTED, &value);
 		}
 		sirq_log = readl(i2c_bus->reg_base + AST2700_I2CC_SIRQ_LOG);
 		target_rx_len = AST2600_I2C_GET_RX_DMA_LEN(readl(i2c_bus->reg_base +
